{#- Source → Output: a card, and the interface it produces, overlapping.

    The argument this page makes is one move long: semantic HTML on the
    left, the interface Cirth renders from it on the right, close enough
    together that the eye reads them as one object. Everything here is in
    service of that and nothing else — including the fact that only one
    of the two is in a frame. The source is a document being shown; the
    output is the thing itself.

    What the composition deliberately does not have: a per-character
    reveal, an erase cycle, a rAF scheduler, output elements gated on
    offsets found in the source text, an IntersectionObserver, a
    focus/selection settler, a `window` test surface to drive it all —
    and, now, any control at all. The build and theme selects that used to
    sit in these two title bands were the last thing here asking to be
    understood before the demo could be: a reader had to work out what
    "Default / Classless / Scoped" meant to know what they were looking
    at. The four builds are a link away in the ledger below, where the
    reader who wants them is already looking for evidence.

    The write-in is a CSS wipe (docs/src/styles/style.css), so the whole
    snippet is in the DOM and complete at every frame — selection, copy,
    assistive technology and no-JS see the same finished source they
    always did, and the panel cannot reflow because nothing about it
    changes. The script below is what is genuinely left: one thing markup
    cannot say, which is that the framed document is a separate document
    and has to be told which colour scheme the page is in. -#}
<div class="docs-lab-stage">
	<section class="docs-plate docs-source-panel" aria-labelledby="source-label">
		<header>
			<span id="source-label">Source</span>
		</header>
		<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html" data-lab-source>{{ labSources.default | highlightHtml | safe }}</code></pre>
	</section>

	{#- The output is not in a panel. It used to be a second .docs-plate
	    with its own "Output" title band, which put the shell's frame
	    around the one thing on this page that is not the shell: a card
	    Cirth has already finished. Two frames, and the inner one — the
	    real one — read as the contents of the outer one. What is left is
	    the rendered page itself, lifted off the source and labelled by
	    the iframe's title rather than by a band above it.

	    tabindex="-1": the frame is a picture of what Cirth renders, not
	    something to operate. Its document is inert and it has nothing to
	    scroll, so the stop Chromium gives every iframe led nowhere and
	    painted no ring. This removes a dead stop; it does not make
	    anything focusable. -#}
	<div class="docs-output-frame">
		<iframe
			title="Authentic Cirth default build rendering a semantic sign-in form"
			src="/lab/default/?theme=light"
			data-lab-frame
			loading="eager"
			tabindex="-1"
		></iframe>
	</div>
</div>

<script>
	(() => {
		const frame = document.querySelector("[data-lab-frame]");
		if (!frame) return;

		// All that is left. The demo has no controls: a reader should be
		// able to take the argument — semantic HTML on one side, the
		// interface Cirth renders from it on the other — without being
		// asked to operate anything first. The one thing that still cannot
		// be expressed in markup is that the framed document is a separate
		// document, so it does not inherit the page's colour scheme; this
		// follows it instead of offering a switch for it. Everything else
		// the switcher used to do is a link away in the ledger below.
		const base = (document.documentElement.dataset.base || "/").replace(/\/$/, "");
		const applyTheme = () => {
			const dark = document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark";
			const next = `${base}/lab/default/?theme=${dark ? "dark" : "light"}`;
			if (frame.getAttribute("src") !== next) frame.setAttribute("src", next);
		};

		applyTheme();
		new MutationObserver(applyTheme).observe(document.documentElement, {
			attributeFilter: ["data-theme"],
		});
	})();
</script>